WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.770
Welcome to digits, zero and positional constraints.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.580
In a digit puzzle, moving just one card can change a

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.950
number's size and whether it passes a rule.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.610
Imagine the places as separate slots,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.210
each with its own job.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.970
Look for clues about the first digit, the last digit,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.630
and whether cards may repeat.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.100
Those restrictions can turn a messy AMC search into

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:23.910
a short list.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.580
In a Rossmoyne style counting puzzle, you can explain

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.010
why your cases cover every possible number.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.290
Use the digit cards zero, four and seven once each.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.630
Make the smallest three-digit number.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:38.350
Where should zero go?

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.960
Decide which place matters most for making

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:41.750
the number small.

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:47.390
The hundreds place has the biggest effect,

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.900
and a three-digit number needs a nonzero digit there.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.920
Four is our smallest choice.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:55.880
Put zero in the tens and seven in the units.

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.560
The number is four hundred and seven.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.140
Putting zero first would leave a two-digit number.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.360
Use zero, two and five once each.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.180
How many three-digit even numbers can you make?

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.590
This time the units place is the one with

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.500
the tightest rule.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.740
Start there and consider each allowed ending separately.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.870
A final zero leaves two and five free to swap,

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.055
making two hundred and fifty and five

00:01:23.055 --> 00:01:23.410
hundred and twenty.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.870
A final two leaves zero and five.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:29.950
Five must come first, giving five hundred and two.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.430
Our two cases give three numbers altogether.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.730
Make a five-digit number from zero, one, two,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.470
four and six, using each card once.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.440
The number must exceed forty thousand and be

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.670
divisible by four.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.330
How many numbers work?

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.950
Split the puzzle by the first card and the final pair.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.060
The first digit is four or six.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.470
Divisibility by four depends on the final pair because

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.540
one hundred is divisible by four.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:04.140
Group the possible endings by their last digit.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.400
Ending in zero gives twenty, forty and sixty.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.280
Ending in two gives twelve.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.500
Ending in four gives zero four,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.560
twenty-four and sixty-four.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.140
Ending in six gives sixteen.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.350
With four first, cross out endings containing four.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:21.890
Four endings remain.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.550
With six first, cross out endings containing six.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.150
Five endings remain.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.290
After choosing the first digit and the ending,

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.170
two cards are left for the middle.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:33.870
They can go in either order.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.890
The first case gives four times two, or eight.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.010
The second gives five times two, or ten.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:43.050
Add them for eighteen numbers.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.460
For example, sixty-one thousand two hundred and four

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.870
ends in zero four.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.650
The hundreds before it make multiples of four,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.490
and so does that final four.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.850
Zero is allowed inside the number or at the end.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.970
It cannot go at the very start of a five-digit number.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.500
Cross out any ending containing the card already used

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.110
at the front.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.880
Finally, remember to count the ways of placing

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.830
the middle cards.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.600
On a timed paper, mark the places with the

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.010
strongest rules first.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.400
Case-split once, use a table if it helps, and check that

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.910
every number sits in exactly one case.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.220
You can use the same method whenever a question combines

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.530
size, divisibility and rules about using digits.
